Z||000000||Uaana||08-21-2000||01:10 AM||||O.k after doing some weekend reading. I came up with a engine question for all you guys.<BR>Has anyone tried to use an 87 GNX engine? From looking at boards like LS1 and the turbo buick boards. Sounds like this is one nasty 6! So has anyone tried to drop this plant into a car that can actually handle? I guess GM also dropped a simailar plant into the Turbo TA.<BR>I have no solid plans as of yet for a transplant, the 2.8 only has 72k on it just doing my research in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroSTETZ||08-21-2000||01:31 AM||||i have asked this question at turbobuick.com a while back, and was told that it would be VERY difficult, especially finding a transmission that would work. my question is, what kind of output could come from a turbocharged 3800, or a 3.4tdc? i saw figures somewhere for 375hp from a 3.4tdc at something like 7300 rpm or something ridiculous like that.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroSTETZ||08-21-2000||01:31 AM||||um, yeah... wasnt a double post, i was- er- um- testing?<BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroSTETZ (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||08-21-2000||01:47 AM||||Well, you'd be putting a RWD engine into a transverse mount application.  So other than the tranny possibly bolting up, absolutely nothing will line up or be easy.  I think it would be equivalent to doing a V8 swap without the aid of a pre-fabbed kit. And the turbo would only complicate things.  Could you imagine the heat build-up back there?  Where would the intercooler go?  The 3.8 turbo might make 350+ hp and a butt load of torque but you'll have a he!! of a time getting it to the ground.  You would have to use a 4T80(northstar) tranny or something.<P>  The series II 3800 s/c is at least a transverse mount engine with readily available swap kits.  Granted it has less hp that the GNX but mods are available.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||08-21-2000||01:49 AM||||In the unlikely event you could snag one of the 509 GNX engines built..........||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-21-2000||02:12 AM||||Uaana--The Grand National motor wasn't even close to the '81 Turbo T/A motor.  The GN was a 3.8/3800 and the Turbo T/A motor was a 301ci (about 5.0 liters).<P>I think the Grand National motors were more streetable than the GNX motors.  More plentiful, too.  terryk is right.  I wouldn't count on finding a true GNX motor in many boneyards.  (Yes.  There is a difference.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||08-21-2000||06:32 AM||||The turbo TA that did have the 3.8 turbo is the 88(?) pace car replica. One bad mama jama!<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||08-21-2000||07:33 AM||||The GNX had the same engine as the GN,it had a different turbo and some suspension change.It had a torque arm on the rear.There were 514 Gn's pulled from the assembly line for the modifications.The GNX was rated at 245hp and the GN was rated at 225hp.My GN has the original engine(231 cubic inches) and has taken my car to 115 MPH in the 1/4.Considering the car weight,it had to put out from 425 to 441hp,wich is the upper limit of the head gaskets(only four head bolts per cylinder)A 425a front wheel drive transmission from an Olds or Caddy would probably work just fine.v8Archie has done one or two of these in the past,but from what I understand he doesn't want to do any more.His was transverse.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||08-21-2000||07:43 AM||||Stimpy ,That was 89,and the turbo Trans Am actually had slightly better heads.Because of the narrower frame rails on the Trans Am ,Pontiac raised the exhaust ports and consequently improved exhaust flow.<BR>Monkeyman,The 3.8 is an off center engine,while the 3800 is an on center engine.The engines are very docile and easy to drive.I get over 20MPG on the highway with mine. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||whamel||08-21-2000||09:02 AM||||When I asked ACE about using my T-Type engine for my swap they told me to try something else as that wouldn't be a good candidate.  They mentioned that emissions would be a problem (not for me though) and that you could get much better performance out of a newer series II 3800.  There were probably some other reasons involved with it as well, but they didn't go into that.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||08-21-2000||06:12 PM||||The series II 3800 is just as strong or stronger as the 3.8 turbo (supercharged anyway). Just use the 3800 with the GNX turbo system and computer.<P>I thought the difference between the GN and GNX was some body and suspension mods and a bigger intercooler.<P>89 T/A turbo pace car heads were actually off a FWD Buick Le Sabre with a few mods.||||reg||1
Z||000011||hugh||08-21-2000||07:40 PM||||The correct number of GNX's is 547. The GNX turbo was ceramic coated to spool faster and they cannot be bought.In order to buy anything specifically GNX related you have to produce proof of ownership(or so I have been lead to believe)The exhaust will have to be custom made because it runs under the rear of the engine and into the turbo just above and in front of the right cylinder head.The 3800 engine should be stronger and the newest engines have a counter rotating shaft internally to counteract torque.   ||||reg||1
